WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: How did you get your first&nbsp;
script in the hands of John Carpenter?

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.320
Jim Agnew, Screenwriter/Producer: Well it was&nbsp;
the first thing I wrote. I’d been working as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.160
a producer on indie films and music videos and&nbsp;
commercials and like I’m going to take the leap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.480
into screenwriting. So I saved enough money for&nbsp;
one year to just work on screenwriting and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.920
this idea to do a very pulp fiction type horror&nbsp;
film, kind of non-linear. I had a friend who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.120
a screenwriter (Sean Keller) who had become my&nbsp;
writing partner and we’d never worked together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.160
we just decided to write it and after three&nbsp;
months just working on the script with like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.320
no expectations we ended up getting a manager&nbsp;
from it because it was so unusual and odd and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.440
different than what was out there and then from&nbsp;
there I get a call a few weeks later saying John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.320
Carpenter read it, loves it! I grew up watching&nbsp;
his movies and by the way he’s the greatest guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.720
on the face of the earth. He’s the coolest dude&nbsp;
you could ever meet. He read the script, liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.520
it and was very interested in directing it and&nbsp;
his process was to sit there with the script and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.680
read it aloud to us page-by-page and everything&nbsp;
that he didn’t like or thought wasn’t good he’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.480
go “Come on, you can do better than that.”&nbsp;
Which was a learning experience in itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.400
and it showed, it taught me a lot about what&nbsp;
directors are looking for and what they want and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.840
he's a great teacher. The film never got made but&nbsp;
it was a really good experience and then from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:32.080
we actually did an uncredited rewrite on his last&nbsp;
film THE WARD, so it worked out great because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.760
we got paid to rewrite the film. So we’ve worked&nbsp;
with Carpenter twice and he’s a really good dude.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.000
Film Courage: And so this was the second&nbsp;
script that you and your writing partner?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.080
Jim: That was actually the first one.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:49.920
Film Courage: Okay, sorry…but how did all&nbsp;
of this get in the hands of John Carpenter?

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.920
Jim: Well I had spent time, the first 10 years of&nbsp;
work in the film industry I worked for production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.520
companies and produced some indie films and&nbsp;
things. So I kind of knew how the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.920
worked a little bit. I had an unfair advantage&nbsp;
to most people just stepping in. But I spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.680
10 years working in the world and making some&nbsp;
connections and also understanding how scripts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.720
work so I really had worked on a few things where&nbsp;
it’s like I could probably write as well as that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.440
or even saw what other people were making and&nbsp;
some other screenplays and was like I think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.760
could do that. So that’s kind of where it&nbsp;
came out of. We also knew that we had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.760
write something that was really unusual and out&nbsp;
of the box. We didn’t write a script to be made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.720
we wrote a script to get noticed and that’s where&nbsp;
I think really it worked. It was like Okay if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.040
gets made, great. But people will not forget&nbsp;
this script and it did what it was supposed to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.160
because it got us a manager and then later it got&nbsp;
us an agent from another script and it just kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.880
the ball rolling and people still asked about&nbsp;
that script and we’ve optioned it I don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.280
maybe six or seven times in the last 15 years.&nbsp;
It still hasn’t been made but it’s done well.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:00.200
Film Courage: When you sat down with John&nbsp;
Carpenter, where was this? An office building?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.680
Jim: Yeah, I’m not going to say where his office&nbsp;
is but he has an old Craftsman. I think maybe he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.120
lived there 20, 30 years ago that he still owns.&nbsp;
It’s been converted into an office. So it’s cool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.080
You go in there and all of his posters are up, all&nbsp;
of his scripts are there. He just sits in the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:22.480
office. Most of the time when we would talk about&nbsp;
the script we would also talk about basketball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.080
and music and all of these other things. He gave&nbsp;
me a lot of advice about the film business. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.960
would say people are going to rip you off, they’re&nbsp;
going to do this, they’re going to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.160
don’t start writing until you get paid, do this&nbsp;
and that. One of the best stories I have about him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.600
is when we were doing a rewrite on THE WARD&nbsp;
for him there was a big conference call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.440
with all of the producers and everybody kept&nbsp;
telling us what to do and he stopped everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.480
and he just said Hey, let them go do what&nbsp;
they do. And it was the coolest thing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.240
we knew all we had to do was just talk to&nbsp;
Carpenter. It’s like I don’t have to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.440
with everybody else and pretty much the script&nbsp;
(the rewrite) we did was the movie that we saw.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.600
Film Courage: And when he started going through&nbsp;
the script and saying you can do better than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.480
did he show you what better was within the script?

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:18.080
Jim: No, he let you…he kind of treated you as a&nbsp;
professional. It’s like this can be better which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.480
means you need to go figure out how to&nbsp;
make it better. And some lines he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.520
like You know a hero would never say that?&nbsp;
The lead of the movie wouldn’t say that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.920
It was a good education in understanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.480
they are not going to do the work for me and&nbsp;
things I didn’t do correctly in the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.800
and that’s not necessarily their job. It’s like&nbsp;
this isn’t working so you need to give me option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:47.760
A, B and C. So that’s a really good lesson from&nbsp;
somebody who knows films backwards and forwards.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.440
Film Courage: So when you and Sean left there&nbsp;
with your [script], whether you put paperclips or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.640
post-it notes or whatever, how are you racking&nbsp;
your brain to ask Why didn’t he like this?

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.800
Jim: No, it was just like I can’t believe&nbsp;
we’re hanging out with John Carpenter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.160
more than anything but he was so…he’s probably&nbsp;
one of the best people I’ve ever met, not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.480
in the film business but just a great person&nbsp;
and super cool and just gives great advice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.280
he didn’t do it because from a place of like&nbsp;
Come On? It was like You can do better and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.240
he would say You can do better. You know what&nbsp;
just really pushing and really dug the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.120
To have someone with the movies you watched&nbsp;
growing up say that it like you better listen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.440
It was part of educating…I still…I’ve been doing&nbsp;
this 15 years professionally as a screenwriter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.360
I’m trying to educate myself all of the time&nbsp;
to get better everyday. So it’s a good lesson.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.400
Film Courage: Jim, were you paid for the script?

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.760
Jim: No that one was just work to try to get it to&nbsp;
the finish line. That happens sometimes. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.680
things are optioned and sometimes things are not.&nbsp;
If you have a good attachment like a director like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.440
him you know you sometimes work on trying to get&nbsp;
to the next level to go out and get the financing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:09.680
So not every job or every project you work on,&nbsp;
you’re not always getting paid. Sometimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.520
have to make that decision, do you want to do the&nbsp;
work to try and get to the next level? Or do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.320
want to try to get paid? Sometimes no one is going&nbsp;
to pay you so it’s like either the market doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.360
want it right now or will want it with attachments&nbsp;
or it’s a tricky thing. Most people in the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.720
business won’t make money unless multiple people&nbsp;
want to buy the project. If just one person wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.440
to make it they’re like sure we will give you&nbsp;
an option, we’ll do this. We may have optioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:41.920
that script. I don’t really remember but it was&nbsp;
never purchased and a lot of times you just get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.840
an option. You don’t get paid for a script. You&nbsp;
read these stories of people selling a script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.720
here or there, that happens. But if you’re more in&nbsp;
the indie world (10 million and under), you option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.960
a script, maybe they give you five grand, maybe&nbsp;
they give you 10 grand, maybe they will give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:00.400
a $100.00 and when the movie gets made, the first&nbsp;
day of shooting is when they actually pay you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.520
the script so want to be a team player and get&nbsp;
the movie made you have to work a lot for free.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.760
Film Courage: So going there you and&nbsp;
Sean both knew we are not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.400
be paid but we see the opportunity down the line?

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.880
Jim: Exactly. It was like having a master class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.760
where the guy is sitting there and you can&nbsp;
ask him anything you want, so yeah it’s great.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.440
Film Courage: What’s the story&nbsp;
behind your first screenplay sale?

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.800
Jim: So the first screenplay sale was the script&nbsp;
we wrote after the John Carpenter project which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.960
called LA GOTHIC. This was called DAMNED and this&nbsp;
script got out and it was picked up by a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.640
big producer and they arranged the financing for&nbsp;
the film and they actually paid for the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.840
What happened was a few days before shooting&nbsp;
it was fully cast…I think they were shooting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.000
like a Monday and this was Friday. The cast&nbsp;
was getting on plane flights to fly out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.640
Missouri and they gets these frantic calls like&nbsp;
don’t get on the plane because the money fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.800
out three days before principal photography&nbsp;
and that happens sometimes in films. Until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.680
you’re…actually I even have the theory that on an&nbsp;
indie film until you’re actually finished shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.920
that’s when you don’t really have to worry&nbsp;
about the money so much. You have to worry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.360
post but I’ve worked on films where sometimes&nbsp;
they don’t have all of the money that they say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:21.440
they do. So it’s a problem that happens but it&nbsp;
was sad, we got paid at least. We got the rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.440
to the script back and they were great to give&nbsp;
us that back because the movie didn’t get made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.000
and we optioned it several more times but it was&nbsp;
very disappointing because that was the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.680
thing I thought was going to get made and it&nbsp;
didn’t. It got very close and it fell apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.960
but you know, you live and learn and then you&nbsp;
move on to the next project and keep going.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.560
Film Courage: So that didn’t deter you?

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:46.160
Jim: No, not at all! No. It actually made&nbsp;
me think even more okay we’ve got to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.080
even harder on the next project and&nbsp;
get something made because it came so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.880
close and finally we’re making some money as&nbsp;
screenwriters and we were like let’s do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.840
No actually…it actually emboldened&nbsp;
me to even go harder at things.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.800
Film Courage: In a case like&nbsp;
that who call the actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.480
the location…is that the producer that&nbsp;
is making all of these horrible calls?

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:08.543
Jim: Yes.

00:09:08.543 --> 00:09:11.600
Film Courage: That’s probably&nbsp;
got to be very difficult and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.400
there was no chance of maybe in a&nbsp;
month…it was basically this is done?

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.920
Jim: They talked about it but usually in&nbsp;
these kind of projects if that happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.040
it’s kind of hard to regroup. You spend so&nbsp;
many months putting the whole team together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.680
doing everything, getting the actors in line,&nbsp;
getting their schedules, making deals and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.400
for it to all go sideways and not know when you’re&nbsp;
going to pick it back up, it’s very difficult. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:40.800
does happen don’t get me wrong but it because&nbsp;
like okay…there’s a little bit of a jinx on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.920
project at that point so you just kind of have&nbsp;
to move on and just try to make the next one.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.720
Film Courage: This was your&nbsp;
first screenplay that you wrote?

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.600
Jim: Second.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:56.640
Film Courage: With Sean…and&nbsp;
with the first one did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.520
expect anything from it (this&nbsp;
was the John Carpenter one)?

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:05.200
Jim: You know the expectations were just to get&nbsp;
noticed, really. Because it was really kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.840
crazy and that’s another thing to talk to a lot of&nbsp;
people about it’s like what are your expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:16.400
with the script? If you write something to sell&nbsp;
it or to get it made or get noticed and a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.920
of young screenwriters I think don’t try to write&nbsp;
something to get noticed. Because if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.560
do this for a living it’s something that you’ve&nbsp;
got to like…you’re not just going to be writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.480
one script, you are going to be writing every&nbsp;
day, all of the time and have tons of scripts.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:34.000
Film Courage: Do you think the chain&nbsp;
of events would have happened today?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.160
Because things have changed so&nbsp;
rapidly in so many ways, so you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.920
that same story could be duplicated? Either&nbsp;
one, whether you get a script that you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.720
you won’t be paid for but into the hands of&nbsp;
some master-class person, basically a master.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:55.200
Jim: Definitely. It happens all of the time. A&nbsp;
lot of people who become big writers they write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.440
a script that turns into a sample and even I have&nbsp;
had that several times in my career where there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.800
like a sample of a horror film that everybody was&nbsp;
into and then a thriller that everybody was into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:10.880
and then I recently wrote a really big action&nbsp;
film that’s getting a lot of traction right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:17.920
but it’s also just in the last few weeks has got&nbsp;
me up for some big writing gigs because if you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.520
trying to get a job for an 80 million dollar&nbsp;
action film, you can’t show them a one million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:27.920
dollar horror film. You have to have that sample&nbsp;
that gets people’s attention and professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.120
high-level producers read, they know by your&nbsp;
sample if you can do the other job. So you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.360
you have to have samples that fit the type of work&nbsp;
you’re looking for. So it was a great example.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.840
Question For The Viewers: Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.003
you have a screenplay sample for&nbsp;
the kind of movies you want to make?

